# Ask when unsure

Guessing wastes a review cycle. When a decision would change the shape of the
result, ask before building.

## Ask when

- Requirements allow several materially different implementations.
- Credentials, hosts, realms, tenants or schemas are unclear or do not work as described.
- Something the user stated does not match what you observe. Report the mismatch
  with evidence and ask, rather than silently working around it.
- Scope is unclear: one iteration or several.

## How to ask

- Use the AskQuestion tool rather than listing options in prose.
- Ask one or two decisive questions at a time, not a questionnaire.
- Do a quick read of the code or a quick query first so the options you offer are
  concrete and informed.
- Recommend an option and say why.

## Do not ask when

- The answer is discoverable from the codebase or a read-only query. Look it up.
- The choice is trivial or easily reversible. Pick a sensible default, state it,
  and move on.

## Example

```
# BAD - guesses, then reports a broken result
Assumes 2-letter country codes, seeds data, reports success.

# GOOD - checks, then asks only what code cannot answer
Queries the country table, finds 3-letter codes with an officeExists flag,
uses them; then asks the user which tenant schema to target, since all four
local schemas hold real-looking data.
```
